1
00:00:00,375 --> 00:00:03,175
Imagine the last ordinary morning on Earth.

2
00:00:03,175 --> 00:00:05,138
There is no asteroid in the sky.

3
00:00:05,138 --> 00:00:06,825
No alien invasion.

4
00:00:06,825 --> 00:00:10,225
The Sun rises exactly where people expect it to rise.

5
00:00:10,225 --> 00:00:13,388
Somewhere, a door opens, and someone steps outside without

6
00:00:13,388 --> 00:00:15,112
carrying their own air.

7
00:00:15,112 --> 00:00:16,938
Perhaps they notice a bird.

8
00:00:16,938 --> 00:00:18,975
Perhaps there are no birds left.

9
00:00:18,975 --> 00:00:21,662
We cannot know what that morning would look like, or whether

10
00:00:21,662 --> 00:00:23,962
anyone like us will be there to see it.

11
00:00:23,962 --> 00:00:27,362
This is an imagined scene, not a prediction of a particular

12
00:00:27,362 --> 00:00:28,775
future family.

13
00:00:29,175 --> 00:00:31,288
But the question behind it is real.

14
00:00:31,288 --> 00:00:33,950
How much longer can Earth remain a home?

15
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:37,300
The answer is not simply, until the Sun dies.

16
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:41,350
Our star does not need to explode to make this world uninhabitable.

17
00:00:41,350 --> 00:00:44,788
It only needs to keep doing what it already does: turning

18
00:00:44,788 --> 00:00:48,475
hydrogen into helium, and slowly changing as a result.

19
00:00:49,150 --> 00:00:52,175
Long before a swollen red Sun reaches toward the inner

20
00:00:52,175 --> 00:00:55,262
planets, Earth could lose the conditions that support

21
00:00:55,262 --> 00:00:57,312
its familiar living world.

22
00:00:57,312 --> 00:01:01,325
Forests, breathable air, oceans, and the last surviving

23
00:01:01,325 --> 00:01:05,488
microbes do not necessarily share an expiration date.

24
00:01:05,488 --> 00:01:08,625
Today, we are going to follow those different clocks,

25
00:01:08,625 --> 00:01:11,675
and ask what it would actually mean to save a planet.

26
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:15,712
Before we travel forward, we need to decide what we are

27
00:01:15,712 --> 00:01:17,338
trying to preserve.

28
00:01:17,338 --> 00:01:20,450
An astronomer may call a planet potentially habitable

29
00:01:20,450 --> 00:01:23,800
if conditions could allow liquid water at its surface.

30
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:26,500
That does not mean a person could walk there.

31
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:30,512
It does not guarantee oxygen, crops, healthy oceans, or

32
00:01:30,512 --> 00:01:31,725
even life.

33
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:34,575
A world can be wet and deadly.

34
00:01:34,575 --> 00:01:37,962
It can contain microbes while being completely inaccessible

35
00:01:37,962 --> 00:01:40,000
to an unprotected human.

36
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,262
It can have a narrow band of tolerable weather while most

37
00:01:43,262 --> 00:01:45,275
of its surface is hostile.

38
00:01:45,275 --> 00:01:48,750
And a technological community might survive inside sealed

39
00:01:48,750 --> 00:01:52,225
structures on a planet whose natural environment has already

40
00:01:52,225 --> 00:01:54,250
become unsuitable for them.

41
00:01:54,725 --> 00:01:57,900
So there are several questions hiding inside our title.

42
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:01,137
When does Earth stop supporting people outdoors?

43
00:02:01,137 --> 00:02:04,050
When do complex ecosystems decline?

44
00:02:04,050 --> 00:02:07,238
When does the atmosphere lose its abundant oxygen?

45
00:02:07,238 --> 00:02:09,275
When do the oceans disappear?

46
00:02:09,275 --> 00:02:12,212
When does the last habitable pocket vanish?

47
00:02:12,212 --> 00:02:15,300
Finally, does the planet itself survive?

48
00:02:15,750 --> 00:02:19,300
Those questions overlap, but they are not interchangeable.

49
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:22,763
Through this journey, watch what each proposed deadline

50
00:02:22,763 --> 00:02:24,338
actually measures.

51
00:02:24,338 --> 00:02:28,288
A number describing the future of land plants is not automatically

52
00:02:28,288 --> 00:02:32,050
a countdown to the death of every organism on Earth.

53
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:37,150
The first clock is the one we live by: decades, centuries,

54
00:02:37,150 --> 00:02:40,050
and the decisions of existing societies.

55
00:02:40,050 --> 00:02:44,300
Human-caused warming, habitat destruction, and other environmental

56
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:46,638
pressures operate on that clock.

57
00:02:46,638 --> 00:02:50,200
Their consequences matter now, without waiting for anything

58
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:52,450
dramatic to happen to the Sun.

59
00:02:52,875 --> 00:02:54,913
The second clock is geological.

60
00:02:54,913 --> 00:02:56,338
Continents move.

61
00:02:56,338 --> 00:02:58,488
Mountains rise and erode.

62
00:02:58,488 --> 00:03:02,188
Carbon passes between rock, ocean, atmosphere, and living

63
00:03:02,188 --> 00:03:06,050
things. Over millions of years, those exchanges help shape

64
00:03:06,050 --> 00:03:09,175
the climate and the resources available to life.

65
00:03:09,625 --> 00:03:12,212
The third clock belongs to the star.

66
00:03:12,212 --> 00:03:15,625
Over hundreds of millions and billions of years, the Sun

67
00:03:15,625 --> 00:03:19,288
evolves. That changes the energy arriving at the top of

68
00:03:19,288 --> 00:03:22,738
Earth's atmosphere, even if no one burns another lump

69
00:03:22,738 --> 00:03:23,800
of coal.

70
00:03:24,250 --> 00:03:26,912
We will spend much of this film on the second and third

71
00:03:26,912 --> 00:03:30,588
clocks. That does not make the first one disappear.

72
00:03:30,588 --> 00:03:34,200
Solar evolution is not the explanation for the rapid warming

73
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:36,112
people are causing today.

74
00:03:36,112 --> 00:03:40,425
Nor does a distant natural limit make present damage unavoidable.

75
00:03:41,025 --> 00:03:44,200
Think of a house on a coast that will eventually change.

76
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:47,800
Knowing the coastline has a long future does not mean

77
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:51,050
it is pointless to repair the roof this afternoon.

78
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:55,162
The Sun is not a ball of ordinary fire.

79
00:03:55,162 --> 00:03:57,588
Fire needs chemical reactions.

80
00:03:57,588 --> 00:04:00,838
The Sun's power comes mainly from nuclear fusion in its

81
00:04:00,838 --> 00:04:04,450
core, where hydrogen is transformed into helium under

82
00:04:04,450 --> 00:04:06,600
enormous pressure and temperature.

83
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:09,288
Gravity pulls the star inward.

84
00:04:09,288 --> 00:04:13,000
The hot interior provides pressure that resists that compression.

85
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,812
The Sun we see is the result of a continually maintained

86
00:04:16,812 --> 00:04:20,850
balance, rather than a solid object with a furnace installed

87
00:04:20,850 --> 00:04:21,975
inside it.

88
00:04:22,425 --> 00:04:25,950
As fusion proceeds, the composition of the core changes.

89
00:04:25,950 --> 00:04:27,800
Helium accumulates.

90
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:30,812
The structure adjusts, and the core becomes hotter and

91
00:04:30,812 --> 00:04:34,488
denser. For a star like ours during this long stage of

92
00:04:34,488 --> 00:04:38,675
its life, those changes gradually increase its luminosity:

93
00:04:38,675 --> 00:04:41,850
the total energy it radiates into space.

94
00:04:42,325 --> 00:04:44,537
That is the counterintuitive part.

95
00:04:44,537 --> 00:04:47,575
A star can become brighter while consuming the fuel that

96
00:04:47,575 --> 00:04:49,700
will eventually transform it.

97
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:52,550
It does not simply dim like a flashlight with a dying

98
00:04:52,550 --> 00:04:53,525
battery.

99
00:04:53,975 --> 00:04:57,400
The changes are slow compared with a human lifetime.

100
00:04:57,400 --> 00:05:00,750
They are powerful compared with the environmental tolerances

101
00:05:00,750 --> 00:05:04,250
of a biosphere. Earth is living beside an engine that

102
00:05:04,250 --> 00:05:07,712
is stable enough to make civilization possible, but not

103
00:05:07,712 --> 00:05:10,250
permanently fixed at today's setting.

104
00:05:11,325 --> 00:05:15,025
A useful rough scale is that the Sun's luminosity increases

105
00:05:15,025 --> 00:05:18,625
by about ten percent over a billion years around its present

106
00:05:18,625 --> 00:05:22,912
stage. Treat that as an approximation, not a universal

107
00:05:22,912 --> 00:05:25,425
linear schedule stretching forever.

108
00:05:25,850 --> 00:05:28,025
Ten percent may sound harmless.

109
00:05:28,025 --> 00:05:31,300
It is not ten percent added to a thermometer reading.

110
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:34,537
It is a change in the incoming power available to warm

111
00:05:34,537 --> 00:05:36,212
an entire planet.

112
00:05:36,212 --> 00:05:40,412
Earth's response depends on clouds, ice, water vapor,

113
00:05:40,412 --> 00:05:44,075
atmospheric gases, circulation, and the way its surface

114
00:05:44,075 --> 00:05:45,500
reflects sunlight.

115
00:05:45,950 --> 00:05:49,000
There is no single knob labeled temperature that follows

116
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,050
solar brightness in a perfectly straight line.

117
00:05:52,050 --> 00:05:54,487
Some responses resist warming.

118
00:05:54,487 --> 00:05:56,150
Others amplify it.

119
00:05:56,150 --> 00:06:00,125
Some change gradually; others can reorganize the climate.

120
00:06:00,725 --> 00:06:04,000
Picture a garden receiving more light every year, except

121
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,737
the years in this thought experiment are millions of years

122
00:06:06,737 --> 00:06:09,888
long. The garden can adjust for a while.

123
00:06:09,888 --> 00:06:12,612
Shade changes. Water moves.

124
00:06:12,612 --> 00:06:15,100
Different species replace the old ones.

125
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:18,562
But adjustment does not guarantee that every useful condition

126
00:06:18,562 --> 00:06:20,475
survives indefinitely.

127
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:24,112
The future is therefore not just a larger Sun drawn above

128
00:06:24,112 --> 00:06:25,712
the same forests.

129
00:06:25,712 --> 00:06:29,112
The forests, atmosphere, and oceans are participants in

130
00:06:29,112 --> 00:06:32,575
the story. They change the way additional sunlight is

131
00:06:32,575 --> 00:06:35,225
received, stored, and released.

132
00:06:36,250 --> 00:06:38,412
There is a clue in Earth's past.

133
00:06:38,412 --> 00:06:41,550
The young Sun was substantially dimmer than it is today,

134
00:06:41,550 --> 00:06:44,462
yet Earth sustained liquid water during much of its

135
00:06:44,462 --> 00:06:48,325
early history. This is often called the faint young Sun

136
00:06:48,325 --> 00:06:49,325
problem.

137
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:54,500
Scientists investigate atmospheric greenhouse gases, clouds,

138
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:57,925
surface reflectivity, and other factors to understand

139
00:06:57,925 --> 00:07:00,112
how the early climate worked.

140
00:07:00,112 --> 00:07:02,862
The details remain an active subject.

141
00:07:02,862 --> 00:07:05,625
The broad lesson is that Earth's temperature has never

142
00:07:05,625 --> 00:07:08,225
depended on solar brightness alone.

143
00:07:08,675 --> 00:07:12,312
A planet can compensate for a changing star if other parts

144
00:07:12,312 --> 00:07:15,462
of its climate system change in the right direction.

145
00:07:15,462 --> 00:07:19,100
In the past, a stronger greenhouse effect helped make

146
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:22,650
a dimmer Sun compatible with an ocean-bearing world.

147
00:07:23,075 --> 00:07:26,475
But the fact that Earth adjusted before is not a promise

148
00:07:26,475 --> 00:07:29,112
of unlimited adjustment in the future.

149
00:07:29,112 --> 00:07:32,712
There is only so much carbon dioxide to remove before

150
00:07:32,712 --> 00:07:36,575
photosynthetic organisms encounter a different problem.

151
00:07:36,575 --> 00:07:39,712
There are only so many ways to rearrange clouds and

152
00:07:39,712 --> 00:07:42,875
water while preserving the familiar biosphere.

153
00:07:43,350 --> 00:07:46,888
Survival through earlier changes tells us that Earth is

154
00:07:46,888 --> 00:07:50,750
dynamic. It does not tell us that Earth has an automatic

155
00:07:50,750 --> 00:07:54,325
commitment to keep conditions comfortable for human beings.

156
00:07:55,525 --> 00:07:58,900
Earth receives sunlight and sends energy back to space,

157
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:01,737
mainly as infrared radiation.

158
00:08:01,737 --> 00:08:05,887
Over a sufficiently long average, a stable climate requires

159
00:08:05,887 --> 00:08:08,925
incoming and outgoing energy to balance.

160
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:12,950
Some sunlight is reflected before it warms the surface.

161
00:08:12,950 --> 00:08:17,500
Clouds, ice, and bright ground contribute to that reflectivity.

162
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:19,825
Much of the rest is absorbed.

163
00:08:19,825 --> 00:08:24,225
Greenhouse gases interact with outgoing infrared radiation

164
00:08:24,225 --> 00:08:27,138
and affect the temperatures needed to release energy back

165
00:08:27,138 --> 00:08:28,425
into space.

166
00:08:28,875 --> 00:08:32,212
This is why a shade and a blanket do different jobs.

167
00:08:32,212 --> 00:08:34,912
A shade reduces incoming sunlight.

168
00:08:34,912 --> 00:08:37,150
A blanket changes heat loss.

169
00:08:37,150 --> 00:08:40,462
Both can alter temperature, but they are not interchangeable

170
00:08:40,462 --> 00:08:42,075
pieces of equipment.

171
00:08:42,525 --> 00:08:46,287
As the Sun brightens, Earth must reflect more, lose more

172
00:08:46,287 --> 00:08:49,787
heat, or get warmer until its energy budget finds a new

173
00:08:49,787 --> 00:08:53,262
balance. Which outcome occurs depends on the changing

174
00:08:53,262 --> 00:08:55,125
atmosphere and surface.

175
00:08:55,575 --> 00:08:58,075
The physics is straightforward enough to describe in a

176
00:08:58,075 --> 00:09:01,362
few sentences. Predicting the response of a planet with

177
00:09:01,362 --> 00:09:05,887
oceans, clouds, continents, chemistry, and life is much

178
00:09:05,887 --> 00:09:09,925
harder. That gap between a clear mechanism and an uncertain

179
00:09:09,925 --> 00:09:13,725
detailed future will appear again and again in our journey.

180
00:09:14,950 --> 00:09:16,650
Now bring in the rocks.

181
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:20,225
Rainwater and carbon dioxide participate in reactions

182
00:09:20,225 --> 00:09:22,287
that weather silicate minerals.

183
00:09:22,287 --> 00:09:25,612
Dissolved products travel toward the ocean, and the wider

184
00:09:25,612 --> 00:09:29,375
carbon cycle can transfer carbon into sediments and rock.

185
00:09:29,375 --> 00:09:33,287
Volcanoes and processes inside Earth return carbon to

186
00:09:33,287 --> 00:09:34,725
the surface system.

187
00:09:35,175 --> 00:09:38,600
This is a simplified picture of a complicated network,

188
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:42,162
not a conveyor belt that removes every carbon atom on

189
00:09:42,162 --> 00:09:45,687
schedule. But it helps explain an important long-term

190
00:09:45,687 --> 00:09:46,775
feedback.

191
00:09:47,225 --> 00:09:50,862
Under suitable conditions, a warmer, wetter climate can

192
00:09:50,862 --> 00:09:54,625
speed up weathering and increase carbon dioxide removal.

193
00:09:54,625 --> 00:09:58,900
Less atmospheric carbon dioxide weakens greenhouse warming,

194
00:09:58,900 --> 00:10:01,812
resisting the initial temperature rise.

195
00:10:01,812 --> 00:10:05,400
A cooler world can shift the balance in the opposite direction.

196
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:09,700
This feedback operates on geological timescales.

197
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:12,900
It is not fast enough to cancel the current human release

198
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:16,925
of greenhouse gases on the timetable that matters to today's

199
00:10:16,925 --> 00:10:18,125
communities.

200
00:10:18,550 --> 00:10:20,137
It also has limits.

201
00:10:20,137 --> 00:10:23,925
Weathering depends on temperature, carbon dioxide, water,

202
00:10:23,925 --> 00:10:27,988
exposed minerals, erosion, and biological activity.

203
00:10:27,988 --> 00:10:30,462
Continents and mountain ranges matter.

204
00:10:30,462 --> 00:10:34,125
The thermostat is made of a planet, not a precision instrument.

205
00:10:34,125 --> 00:10:37,687
Its future behavior is one of the main reasons scientists

206
00:10:37,687 --> 00:10:40,500
obtain different estimates for the lifetime of Earth's

207
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:41,675
biosphere.

208
00:10:42,725 --> 00:10:44,287
Here is the twist.

209
00:10:44,287 --> 00:10:47,925
Carbon dioxide can help make the present climate dangerously

210
00:10:47,925 --> 00:10:51,325
warmer when its concentration rises rapidly.

211
00:10:51,325 --> 00:10:54,950
But plants also need carbon dioxide as a raw material

212
00:10:54,950 --> 00:10:56,550
for photosynthesis.

213
00:10:57,025 --> 00:10:59,737
Those statements do not contradict each other.

214
00:10:59,737 --> 00:11:02,712
A substance can be necessary for life and harmful when

215
00:11:02,712 --> 00:11:05,775
its abundance changes the environment too much.

216
00:11:05,775 --> 00:11:07,487
Water is necessary.

217
00:11:07,487 --> 00:11:10,125
A flood can still destroy a field.

218
00:11:10,575 --> 00:11:14,112
As the Sun brightens over geological time, the carbon

219
00:11:14,112 --> 00:11:18,075
cycle may compensate by drawing atmospheric carbon dioxide

220
00:11:18,075 --> 00:11:22,125
downward. In some models, that eventually becomes a crisis

221
00:11:22,125 --> 00:11:26,250
for plants. The climate's attempt to limit warming reduces

222
00:11:26,250 --> 00:11:29,850
the supply of an ingredient used to build living tissue.

223
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:33,487
Imagine a greenhouse where the lights become progressively

224
00:11:33,487 --> 00:11:37,650
stronger while the carbon supply is progressively restricted.

225
00:11:37,650 --> 00:11:40,825
The plants are not simply receiving a helpful increase

226
00:11:40,825 --> 00:11:45,125
in sunshine. Their whole operating environment is changing.

227
00:11:45,725 --> 00:11:49,000
This is one proposed route toward the decline of complex

228
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:53,287
land life: not an immediate firestorm, but a long tightening

229
00:11:53,287 --> 00:11:56,925
of the conditions that allow primary producers to function.

230
00:11:56,925 --> 00:12:00,387
To understand that possibility, we have to look beyond

231
00:12:00,387 --> 00:12:04,100
the familiar image of a wilting forest and ask how different

232
00:12:04,100 --> 00:12:06,575
plants actually obtain their carbon.

233
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:10,975
Plants have evolved different photosynthetic strategies.

234
00:12:10,975 --> 00:12:14,862
Most familiar trees use a pathway called C three.

235
00:12:14,862 --> 00:12:18,112
Other plants use a carbon-concentrating strategy called

236
00:12:18,112 --> 00:12:22,525
C four, which can offer advantages in certain hot environments

237
00:12:22,525 --> 00:12:26,450
and at relatively low carbon dioxide concentrations.

238
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:29,950
These categories are not a simple ranking from weak to

239
00:12:29,950 --> 00:12:34,425
invincible. Water availability, nutrients, temperature,

240
00:12:34,425 --> 00:12:37,300
and the costs of the mechanisms all matter.

241
00:12:37,300 --> 00:12:40,700
Different species have their own limits, and ecosystems

242
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:43,900
depend on interactions among many organisms.

243
00:12:44,375 --> 00:12:47,750
In a changing future, some vegetation could disappear

244
00:12:47,750 --> 00:12:49,812
while other kinds persist.

245
00:12:49,812 --> 00:12:53,200
A forest need not become a completely sterile desert in

246
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:56,625
one step. It might first become a different landscape,

247
00:12:56,625 --> 00:12:59,387
supporting different animals and a different cycling

248
00:12:59,387 --> 00:13:00,500
of carbon.

249
00:13:00,950 --> 00:13:03,562
The same caution applies to the sea.

250
00:13:03,562 --> 00:13:07,400
Marine photosynthesis has its own organisms, chemical

251
00:13:07,400 --> 00:13:09,462
setting, and constraints.

252
00:13:09,462 --> 00:13:13,075
The disappearance of familiar land plants would not automatically

253
00:13:13,075 --> 00:13:17,025
switch off every source of biological production everywhere.

254
00:13:17,650 --> 00:13:20,925
This is why a convincing account of Earth's future cannot

255
00:13:20,925 --> 00:13:24,362
put one date over a dying tree and label it the end of

256
00:13:24,362 --> 00:13:28,000
life. We need to know which organisms the calculation

257
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,850
represents, which environmental limits it includes, and

258
00:13:31,850 --> 00:13:34,350
how the rest of the system responds.

259
00:13:35,425 --> 00:13:39,725
Some earlier scenarios placed severe carbon dioxide starvation

260
00:13:39,725 --> 00:13:43,275
for land plants around a billion years into the future.

261
00:13:43,275 --> 00:13:45,625
But that is not the final word.

262
00:13:46,075 --> 00:13:49,550
A study by Graham, Halevy, and Abbot, posted in twenty

263
00:13:49,550 --> 00:13:52,875
twenty-four and accepted for publication, reexamined the

264
00:13:52,875 --> 00:13:56,950
relationship between weathering, climate, and plant productivity.

265
00:13:56,950 --> 00:14:00,062
Under some of their assumptions, the long decline in

266
00:14:00,062 --> 00:14:04,187
carbon dioxide can slow and temporarily reverse.

267
00:14:04,187 --> 00:14:07,825
Plants then avoid the earlier starvation scenario.

268
00:14:08,300 --> 00:14:11,612
In those calculations, land plants can persist for roughly

269
00:14:11,612 --> 00:14:15,650
one point six to one point eight six billion years, until

270
00:14:15,650 --> 00:14:19,150
extreme temperatures stop photosynthesis instead.

271
00:14:19,150 --> 00:14:22,562
Those are model results under particular assumptions.

272
00:14:22,562 --> 00:14:25,475
They are not a newly discovered universal date for the

273
00:14:25,475 --> 00:14:26,600
end of Earth.

274
00:14:27,025 --> 00:14:30,100
The important change is not just a later number.

275
00:14:30,100 --> 00:14:33,175
It is a different proposed failure mechanism.

276
00:14:33,175 --> 00:14:36,175
Change how weathering responds to temperature and carbon

277
00:14:36,175 --> 00:14:39,687
dioxide, and you can change both the deadline and the

278
00:14:39,687 --> 00:14:41,950
reason the biosphere reaches it.

279
00:14:42,400 --> 00:14:44,537
That is science doing its job.

280
00:14:44,537 --> 00:14:47,575
A forecast about a coupled planetary system should be

281
00:14:47,575 --> 00:14:50,312
revised when its assumptions improve.

282
00:14:50,312 --> 00:14:53,725
The uncertainty belongs in the story, rather than hidden

283
00:14:53,725 --> 00:14:56,400
behind a dramatic countdown clock.

284
00:14:57,450 --> 00:15:00,525
There is another deadline that does not require the ocean

285
00:15:00,525 --> 00:15:04,850
to boil away. Earth's oxygen-rich atmosphere is maintained

286
00:15:04,850 --> 00:15:09,325
by biology interacting with geological and chemical processes.

287
00:15:09,325 --> 00:15:12,362
It is not a permanent gift that the planet received

288
00:15:12,362 --> 00:15:14,250
once and keeps forever.

289
00:15:14,725 --> 00:15:17,950
In a twenty twenty-one study, Ozaki and Reinhard used

290
00:15:17,950 --> 00:15:21,950
a coupled biogeochemistry and climate model to investigate

291
00:15:21,950 --> 00:15:25,262
how long oxygen-rich conditions could persist.

292
00:15:25,262 --> 00:15:28,800
Their reported mean was about one point zero eight billion

293
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:32,537
years, with a model spread of about zero point one four

294
00:15:32,537 --> 00:15:33,950
billion years.

295
00:15:34,400 --> 00:15:37,862
Their threshold was oxygen above one percent of the present

296
00:15:37,862 --> 00:15:39,450
atmospheric level.

297
00:15:39,450 --> 00:15:40,975
Notice the wording.

298
00:15:40,975 --> 00:15:44,425
One percent of today's oxygen level is not an atmosphere

299
00:15:44,425 --> 00:15:48,375
containing one percent oxygen by volume, and it is certainly

300
00:15:48,375 --> 00:15:50,675
not breathable air for humans.

301
00:15:51,150 --> 00:15:54,625
In their model, the major deoxygenation occurs before

302
00:15:54,625 --> 00:15:57,262
extensive loss of surface water.

303
00:15:57,262 --> 00:16:00,425
So a future observer might find an ocean-bearing Earth

304
00:16:00,425 --> 00:16:04,650
whose atmosphere no longer supports animals like us outdoors.

305
00:16:05,275 --> 00:16:08,850
This result and the newer plant calculations are not chapters

306
00:16:08,850 --> 00:16:11,037
from one agreed simulation.

307
00:16:11,037 --> 00:16:13,850
They use different assumptions and investigate different

308
00:16:13,850 --> 00:16:17,750
outcomes. We cannot simply paste their dates onto one

309
00:16:17,750 --> 00:16:20,575
perfectly synchronized future calendar.

310
00:16:21,600 --> 00:16:24,887
For a person standing on the ground, oxygen is only part

311
00:16:24,887 --> 00:16:28,375
of the problem. Breathing depends on its partial pressure,

312
00:16:28,375 --> 00:16:31,875
which combines the oxygen fraction with the total atmospheric

313
00:16:31,875 --> 00:16:36,500
pressure. Temperature, toxic gases, water, and food matter

314
00:16:36,500 --> 00:16:37,350
too.

315
00:16:37,775 --> 00:16:41,200
For the planet, atmospheric oxygen reflects a balance

316
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:43,325
between sources and sinks.

317
00:16:43,325 --> 00:16:47,475
Photosynthesis produces oxygen, but respiration, decay,

318
00:16:47,475 --> 00:16:50,900
and reactions with reduced materials consume it.

319
00:16:50,900 --> 00:16:54,087
Long-term accumulation involves the fate of carbon and

320
00:16:54,087 --> 00:16:57,537
other elements, including what becomes buried rather than

321
00:16:57,537 --> 00:16:59,425
immediately recycled.

322
00:16:59,875 --> 00:17:03,325
That means a reduction in the oxygen supply can interact

323
00:17:03,325 --> 00:17:05,762
with continuing chemical demand.

324
00:17:05,762 --> 00:17:09,075
The future atmosphere is not a sealed tank that stays

325
00:17:09,075 --> 00:17:11,825
full until a final tree falls.

326
00:17:12,325 --> 00:17:16,012
Lower oxygen would also change atmospheric chemistry and

327
00:17:16,012 --> 00:17:18,425
the protective ozone environment.

328
00:17:18,425 --> 00:17:21,137
Different organisms would face different combinations

329
00:17:21,137 --> 00:17:25,050
of stress. Some microbes can live without oxygen.

330
00:17:25,050 --> 00:17:28,762
Large oxygen-dependent animals cannot simply adopt the

331
00:17:28,762 --> 00:17:30,250
same lifestyle.

332
00:17:30,725 --> 00:17:34,175
A blue planet seen from space can therefore conceal a

333
00:17:34,175 --> 00:17:36,962
profound biological transformation.

334
00:17:36,962 --> 00:17:40,262
Even if water remains visible, the conditions that made

335
00:17:40,262 --> 00:17:43,487
it home for our kind may already be gone.

336
00:17:43,487 --> 00:17:46,725
Color alone does not tell us whether a world welcomes

337
00:17:46,725 --> 00:17:47,725
a breath.

338
00:17:48,750 --> 00:17:52,200
If primary production declines, the consequences spread

339
00:17:52,200 --> 00:17:53,775
through food webs.

340
00:17:53,775 --> 00:17:58,137
Leaves, seeds, algae, and microscopic producers support

341
00:17:58,137 --> 00:18:01,137
organisms that support other organisms.

342
00:18:01,137 --> 00:18:04,137
A predator does not need to be directly harmed by low

343
00:18:04,137 --> 00:18:08,300
carbon dioxide to suffer when its prey loses a food source.

344
00:18:08,850 --> 00:18:11,225
But the response would not be uniform.

345
00:18:11,225 --> 00:18:13,675
Some food webs could reorganize.

346
00:18:13,675 --> 00:18:16,862
Some habitats could retain productivity longer.

347
00:18:16,862 --> 00:18:20,350
Extinction and adaptation would unfold across a changing

348
00:18:20,350 --> 00:18:24,525
geography, rather than follow a single worldwide queue.

349
00:18:25,150 --> 00:18:28,387
Think about the services a living landscape performs without

350
00:18:28,387 --> 00:18:30,837
invoices or replacement parts.

351
00:18:30,837 --> 00:18:33,862
Soil formation. Nutrient recycling.

352
00:18:33,862 --> 00:18:36,587
Pollination. Water regulation.

353
00:18:36,587 --> 00:18:38,262
The movement of carbon.

354
00:18:38,262 --> 00:18:41,562
Relationships between organisms that have evolved together

355
00:18:41,562 --> 00:18:43,900
over immense stretches of time.

356
00:18:44,375 --> 00:18:48,087
A future technological society might produce food in controlled

357
00:18:48,087 --> 00:18:51,300
environments. That could help preserve people.

358
00:18:51,300 --> 00:18:54,287
It would not automatically replace an entire functioning

359
00:18:54,287 --> 00:18:59,250
forest, including its fungi, insects, microbes, seasons,

360
00:18:59,250 --> 00:19:01,650
and evolutionary possibilities.

361
00:19:02,100 --> 00:19:05,087
The distinction matters when we ask whether humanity could

362
00:19:05,087 --> 00:19:07,812
handle the loss of a habitable Earth.

363
00:19:07,812 --> 00:19:12,150
Saving a population, saving selected species, and preserving

364
00:19:12,150 --> 00:19:16,225
an open, self-sustaining biosphere are different projects.

365
00:19:16,225 --> 00:19:18,900
We should not call one of them a complete substitute

366
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:20,075
for the others.

367
00:19:21,125 --> 00:19:24,175
Any detailed illustration of Earth a billion years from

368
00:19:24,175 --> 00:19:25,937
now is speculative.

369
00:19:25,937 --> 00:19:28,662
Continents will not keep today's positions.

370
00:19:28,662 --> 00:19:32,725
Coastlines, mountain ranges, ocean circulation, and the

371
00:19:32,725 --> 00:19:35,175
distribution of rain will change.

372
00:19:35,625 --> 00:19:39,075
That is important because climate is experienced locally.

373
00:19:39,075 --> 00:19:42,687
A global average does not describe a shaded valley,

374
00:19:42,687 --> 00:19:46,487
a humid coastline, a high plateau, and the center of a

375
00:19:46,487 --> 00:19:48,825
dry continent equally well.

376
00:19:48,825 --> 00:19:52,175
Geography can create refuges or remove them.

377
00:19:52,625 --> 00:19:55,837
Our pictures of future landscapes are therefore examples

378
00:19:55,837 --> 00:20:00,475
of processes: vegetation retreating, water becoming scarce,

379
00:20:00,475 --> 00:20:02,712
communities using shelter.

380
00:20:02,712 --> 00:20:05,500
They are not maps of where a particular country will be

381
00:20:05,500 --> 00:20:07,550
safe in a particular year.

382
00:20:08,025 --> 00:20:11,337
Even a familiar rule like move toward the poles can become

383
00:20:11,337 --> 00:20:16,475
too simple. Seasonal light, available land, changing circulation,

384
00:20:16,475 --> 00:20:19,750
water supplies, and the demands of different organisms

385
00:20:19,750 --> 00:20:21,087
would all matter.

386
00:20:21,087 --> 00:20:25,450
A refuge is a combination of conditions, not just a latitude.

387
00:20:26,075 --> 00:20:29,162
The future Earth would not be today's world with a warmer

388
00:20:29,162 --> 00:20:33,100
color filter. Its geography would be part of the mechanism

389
00:20:33,100 --> 00:20:35,437
deciding where life could persist.

390
00:20:35,437 --> 00:20:38,712
Trying to identify the last comfortable beach on a map

391
00:20:38,712 --> 00:20:42,250
of the present would give a precision we do not possess.

392
00:20:43,475 --> 00:20:47,087
Humans cool themselves partly by evaporating sweat.

393
00:20:47,087 --> 00:20:50,112
That works only if the environment allows the body to

394
00:20:50,112 --> 00:20:52,262
transfer enough heat outward.

395
00:20:52,262 --> 00:20:55,800
High humidity can make evaporation less effective, even

396
00:20:55,800 --> 00:20:58,925
before the air temperature reaches the body's internal

397
00:20:58,925 --> 00:20:59,975
temperature.

398
00:21:00,450 --> 00:21:03,675
Researchers use several measures to assess heat stress.

399
00:21:03,675 --> 00:21:06,600
One is wet-bulb temperature, which reflects the combined

400
00:21:06,600 --> 00:21:08,725
influence of heat and moisture.

401
00:21:08,725 --> 00:21:12,225
There is no single universal cutoff that guarantees survival

402
00:21:12,225 --> 00:21:14,387
below it or death above it.

403
00:21:14,387 --> 00:21:18,962
Sunlight, airflow, clothing, activity, age, health, and

404
00:21:18,962 --> 00:21:21,650
exposure duration change the outcome.

405
00:21:22,075 --> 00:21:25,562
People can become dangerously overheated under conditions

406
00:21:25,562 --> 00:21:28,837
well short of a famous theoretical limit.

407
00:21:28,837 --> 00:21:32,187
A future story should not present one number as a magical

408
00:21:32,187 --> 00:21:35,200
boundary around human habitability.

409
00:21:35,700 --> 00:21:38,825
Nor is the only relevant question whether a healthy resting

410
00:21:38,825 --> 00:21:41,212
adult can survive an afternoon.

411
00:21:41,212 --> 00:21:45,125
Can crops grow? Can workers maintain the water system?

412
00:21:45,125 --> 00:21:48,675
Does a power failure turn a home into an emergency?

413
00:21:48,675 --> 00:21:51,950
Can a community support children, older people, and those

414
00:21:51,950 --> 00:21:53,150
who are ill?

415
00:21:53,625 --> 00:21:57,075
A landscape can become impractical for a society long

416
00:21:57,075 --> 00:22:01,300
before every individual exposure is immediately fatal.

417
00:22:01,300 --> 00:22:05,387
Habitability is about sustaining lives, not merely delaying

418
00:22:05,387 --> 00:22:07,375
the moment a body fails.

419
00:22:08,425 --> 00:22:12,012
As the planet warms, water vapor becomes increasingly

420
00:22:12,012 --> 00:22:15,662
important. It is itself a greenhouse gas.

421
00:22:15,662 --> 00:22:19,200
More evaporation can increase atmospheric water vapor,

422
00:22:19,200 --> 00:22:22,225
amplifying warming under many conditions.

423
00:22:22,700 --> 00:22:26,000
Normally, much of the atmosphere's water is kept low down

424
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:29,662
because rising air cools and water condenses.

425
00:22:29,662 --> 00:22:32,737
The upper atmosphere is comparatively dry.

426
00:22:32,737 --> 00:22:35,600
This helps limit the amount of water reaching regions

427
00:22:35,600 --> 00:22:39,125
where sunlight can break its molecules apart and hydrogen

428
00:22:39,125 --> 00:22:40,800
can escape to space.

429
00:22:41,275 --> 00:22:44,537
In a moist greenhouse state, the upper atmosphere becomes

430
00:22:44,537 --> 00:22:46,312
substantially wetter.

431
00:22:46,312 --> 00:22:49,662
That can open a route toward major water loss over long

432
00:22:49,662 --> 00:22:53,562
periods. A moist greenhouse is not the same thing as every

433
00:22:53,562 --> 00:22:56,100
ocean instantly turning to steam.

434
00:22:56,575 --> 00:23:00,125
A runaway greenhouse refers to a more severe energy-balance

435
00:23:00,125 --> 00:23:03,562
problem, where additional warming cannot restore balance

436
00:23:03,562 --> 00:23:07,000
through the usual increase in outgoing thermal radiation

437
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:08,987
across a relevant range.

438
00:23:08,987 --> 00:23:10,750
The distinction matters.

439
00:23:10,750 --> 00:23:14,550
Different models can reach moist conditions, severe heating,

440
00:23:14,550 --> 00:23:17,475
or runaway behavior at different stages.

441
00:23:17,900 --> 00:23:21,475
Clouds, circulation, and atmospheric assumptions affect

442
00:23:21,475 --> 00:23:24,800
the thresholds. We can explain the mechanisms without

443
00:23:24,800 --> 00:23:28,225
pretending that scientists have scheduled the first afternoon

444
00:23:28,225 --> 00:23:29,937
of a moist greenhouse.

445
00:23:29,937 --> 00:23:33,812
The process is physical; the exact timetable remains a

446
00:23:33,812 --> 00:23:35,275
modeling problem.

447
00:23:36,325 --> 00:23:39,350
A disappearing ocean sounds like a drain opening beneath

448
00:23:39,350 --> 00:23:43,300
the seafloor. The long-term escape mechanism is different.

449
00:23:43,300 --> 00:23:45,637
Water enters the atmosphere.

450
00:23:45,637 --> 00:23:49,212
High-energy sunlight can break water molecules apart.

451
00:23:49,212 --> 00:23:53,125
Hydrogen, being light, can escape more readily than oxygen.

452
00:23:53,750 --> 00:23:57,050
Oxygen left behind can participate in reactions with the

453
00:23:57,050 --> 00:24:00,375
surface and atmosphere, and some can be lost through other

454
00:24:00,375 --> 00:24:04,037
processes. The final atmosphere depends on the balance

455
00:24:04,037 --> 00:24:05,562
of those pathways.

456
00:24:05,562 --> 00:24:10,325
You cannot infer a safe, oxygen-rich world simply by separating

457
00:24:10,325 --> 00:24:13,400
the hydrogen from a global ocean on paper.

458
00:24:13,900 --> 00:24:17,087
Water can shift between reservoirs before it is lost to

459
00:24:17,087 --> 00:24:22,225
space. Ice, atmosphere, surface water, and minerals complicate

460
00:24:22,225 --> 00:24:26,250
the accounting. The pace of escape also depends on conditions

461
00:24:26,250 --> 00:24:29,225
in the upper atmosphere and the evolving star.

462
00:24:29,675 --> 00:24:32,900
The key point is that a planet can lose water molecule

463
00:24:32,900 --> 00:24:36,200
by molecule over immense spans of time.

464
00:24:36,200 --> 00:24:39,112
It does not need an explosion that throws the sea into

465
00:24:39,112 --> 00:24:41,225
space in a single wave.

466
00:24:41,675 --> 00:24:44,850
This makes the story less like an action movie and more

467
00:24:44,850 --> 00:24:47,437
like a long failure of retention.

468
00:24:47,437 --> 00:24:51,400
A world that once kept an ocean eventually loses the conditions

469
00:24:51,400 --> 00:24:54,275
that allowed it to remain a world of oceans.

470
00:24:55,375 --> 00:24:59,575
Venus makes the danger easier to picture: a dense atmosphere,

471
00:24:59,575 --> 00:25:03,675
enormous surface heat, and no Earth-like surface ocean.

472
00:25:03,675 --> 00:25:06,862
It shows that a rocky planet near the Sun can have a

473
00:25:06,862 --> 00:25:09,400
radically different climate from ours.

474
00:25:09,825 --> 00:25:13,037
But Venus is not a verified photograph of Earth's exact

475
00:25:13,037 --> 00:25:18,300
future. Its history, rotation, atmosphere, water inventory,

476
00:25:18,300 --> 00:25:22,150
and interactions between interior and surface are different.

477
00:25:22,150 --> 00:25:25,650
Scientists still investigate how much water Venus once

478
00:25:25,650 --> 00:25:28,450
had and how its climate evolved.

479
00:25:28,925 --> 00:25:32,150
Saying Earth will become exactly like Venus can hide more

480
00:25:32,150 --> 00:25:33,662
than it explains.

481
00:25:33,662 --> 00:25:36,800
Our future atmosphere would be shaped by its own carbon

482
00:25:36,800 --> 00:25:41,600
reservoirs, water loss, geological activity, and the timing

483
00:25:41,600 --> 00:25:43,350
of solar evolution.

484
00:25:43,350 --> 00:25:46,587
We should not copy Venus's present pressure and composition

485
00:25:46,587 --> 00:25:49,800
into a forecast without doing the calculation.

486
00:25:50,250 --> 00:25:54,275
The useful comparison is a question about planetary stability.

487
00:25:54,275 --> 00:25:57,887
What combinations of sunlight, atmosphere, and water

488
00:25:57,887 --> 00:25:59,862
allow a temperate surface?

489
00:25:59,862 --> 00:26:02,500
What pushes a world out of that regime?

490
00:26:02,500 --> 00:26:06,125
Which changes can be reversed, and which remove resources

491
00:26:06,125 --> 00:26:07,950
that are difficult to recover?

492
00:26:08,450 --> 00:26:12,000
Venus reminds us that Earth-like size does not guarantee

493
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:13,575
an Earth-like home.

494
00:26:13,575 --> 00:26:16,700
It does not give us a shortcut around the science of how

495
00:26:16,700 --> 00:26:18,850
our own planet will change.

496
00:26:19,900 --> 00:26:23,637
If complex surface ecosystems disappear, the story of

497
00:26:23,637 --> 00:26:25,337
life may continue.

498
00:26:25,337 --> 00:26:29,050
Microorganisms already inhabit environments far removed

499
00:26:29,050 --> 00:26:33,187
from the conditions humans need: beneath the ground, inside

500
00:26:33,187 --> 00:26:36,750
rocks, and around sources of chemical energy.

501
00:26:37,225 --> 00:26:40,237
Some communities do not rely directly on sunlight in the

502
00:26:40,237 --> 00:26:42,525
way a forest does.

503
00:26:42,525 --> 00:26:46,175
Water, chemical gradients, suitable temperatures, and

504
00:26:46,175 --> 00:26:50,150
access to energy can allow biological activity in places

505
00:26:50,150 --> 00:26:52,325
that would appear barren from above.

506
00:26:52,775 --> 00:26:56,800
That does not make the deep subsurface an unlimited sanctuary.

507
00:26:56,800 --> 00:26:58,925
Heat can become excessive.

508
00:26:58,925 --> 00:27:02,800
Water and useful chemical energy can become unavailable.

509
00:27:02,800 --> 00:27:06,887
A microbial refuge must still satisfy the physical requirements

510
00:27:06,887 --> 00:27:08,950
of the organisms living there.

511
00:27:09,400 --> 00:27:12,600
We do not know which place would host Earth's final life,

512
00:27:12,600 --> 00:27:15,662
or when its last population would disappear.

513
00:27:15,662 --> 00:27:19,250
A tiny wet fracture in rock is a plausible kind of setting

514
00:27:19,250 --> 00:27:23,000
to consider, not an identified future address.

515
00:27:23,475 --> 00:27:25,275
Imagine the contrast.

516
00:27:25,275 --> 00:27:28,712
The planet might no longer have trees, birds, or open-air

517
00:27:28,712 --> 00:27:32,412
cities, yet still contain living chemistry behind a mineral

518
00:27:32,412 --> 00:27:36,500
surface. From a human point of view, the world has ended.

519
00:27:36,500 --> 00:27:39,600
From the point of view of life as a broader phenomenon,

520
00:27:39,600 --> 00:27:43,250
a much smaller and stranger chapter remains open.

521
00:27:44,300 --> 00:27:47,637
The phrase last day makes us imagine witnesses gathered

522
00:27:47,637 --> 00:27:52,250
on a shoreline. But ecological endings are usually distributed.

523
00:27:52,250 --> 00:27:55,625
The final member of one population may disappear while

524
00:27:55,625 --> 00:27:58,038
another survives somewhere else.

525
00:27:58,038 --> 00:28:01,737
A region can become hostile, recover temporarily, and

526
00:28:01,737 --> 00:28:03,375
later fail again.

527
00:28:03,825 --> 00:28:07,837
The last natural human habitat, the last forest, and the

528
00:28:07,837 --> 00:28:11,525
last microbial community would not necessarily vanish

529
00:28:11,525 --> 00:28:15,637
together. Some thresholds could be geologically abrupt

530
00:28:15,637 --> 00:28:18,825
while still taking longer than the lifetime of any person

531
00:28:18,825 --> 00:28:20,450
who watched the beginning.

532
00:28:20,900 --> 00:28:24,150
If intelligent descendants exist, their interventions

533
00:28:24,150 --> 00:28:26,275
could also change the sequence.

534
00:28:26,275 --> 00:28:29,775
If they do not exist, there may be no observer to label

535
00:28:29,775 --> 00:28:33,237
an ending. Nature does not issue a certificate when a

536
00:28:33,237 --> 00:28:37,200
planet crosses from rich biosphere to scattered refuges.

537
00:28:37,825 --> 00:28:41,125
So our imagined last ordinary morning is a way into the

538
00:28:41,125 --> 00:28:44,825
question, not a claim that the future has one decisive

539
00:28:44,825 --> 00:28:46,450
cinematic moment.

540
00:28:46,450 --> 00:28:49,425
Much of what is precious could disappear through a succession

541
00:28:49,425 --> 00:28:51,325
of smaller departures.

542
00:28:51,800 --> 00:28:53,737
A song no longer heard.

543
00:28:53,737 --> 00:28:56,987
A reproductive season that no longer succeeds.

544
00:28:56,987 --> 00:29:00,537
A region where breathing outdoors becomes impossible.

545
00:29:00,537 --> 00:29:03,875
The ending of a world can be made of many endings, each

546
00:29:03,875 --> 00:29:06,550
complete for the lives that depended on it.

547
00:29:07,600 --> 00:29:10,775
Eventually, the Sun will exhaust the hydrogen available

548
00:29:10,775 --> 00:29:13,225
for sustained fusion in its core.

549
00:29:13,225 --> 00:29:15,887
Its structure will change dramatically.

550
00:29:15,887 --> 00:29:19,187
Hydrogen burning in a shell around the evolving core and

551
00:29:19,187 --> 00:29:22,850
later stages of stellar evolution will transform it into

552
00:29:22,850 --> 00:29:24,275
a giant star.

553
00:29:24,775 --> 00:29:28,137
NASA describes the Sun as having roughly another five

554
00:29:28,137 --> 00:29:32,200
billion years before entering its red giant future.

555
00:29:32,200 --> 00:29:35,837
That is an approximate evolutionary milestone, not the

556
00:29:35,837 --> 00:29:40,162
date of Earth's first habitability crisis, and not necessarily

557
00:29:40,162 --> 00:29:42,875
the moment of maximum stellar size.

558
00:29:43,350 --> 00:29:46,900
During the giant stages, the Sun's outer layers expand

559
00:29:46,900 --> 00:29:50,500
enormously. Its surface can become cooler in temperature

560
00:29:50,500 --> 00:29:53,950
while its total luminosity becomes much greater, because

561
00:29:53,950 --> 00:29:57,075
the radiating surface is so much larger.

562
00:29:57,075 --> 00:29:59,100
Redder does not mean safe.

563
00:29:59,575 --> 00:30:02,162
Mercury and Venus face destruction.

564
00:30:02,162 --> 00:30:05,025
The consequences for Earth depend on more than simply

565
00:30:05,025 --> 00:30:08,687
drawing a large circle around today's solar system.

566
00:30:08,687 --> 00:30:13,225
The Sun loses mass, planetary orbits respond, and tides

567
00:30:13,225 --> 00:30:15,675
can exchange angular momentum.

568
00:30:16,100 --> 00:30:19,387
By this stage, the familiar biosphere would already be

569
00:30:19,387 --> 00:30:23,175
ancient history under ordinary natural evolution.

570
00:30:23,175 --> 00:30:26,012
Waiting for the red giant is like dating the loss of a

571
00:30:26,012 --> 00:30:29,437
house to the demolition of its foundations, after the

572
00:30:29,437 --> 00:30:34,375
water, electricity, roof, and rooms have long since disappeared.

573
00:30:35,600 --> 00:30:39,512
As the Sun loses mass, Earth's orbit can expand.

574
00:30:39,512 --> 00:30:41,700
That sounds like an escape route.

575
00:30:41,700 --> 00:30:45,450
But a giant star can also exert tidal effects that remove

576
00:30:45,450 --> 00:30:49,475
orbital angular momentum, pulling a planet inward relative

577
00:30:49,475 --> 00:30:52,075
to that expanding-star environment.

578
00:30:52,550 --> 00:30:56,187
A widely cited calculation by Schroder and Smith found

579
00:30:56,187 --> 00:30:59,100
that Earth would be engulfed near the tip of the red giant

580
00:30:59,100 --> 00:31:03,012
branch, about seven point six billion years from now in

581
00:31:03,012 --> 00:31:07,187
their model. Their result included stellar mass loss and

582
00:31:07,187 --> 00:31:08,850
tidal interaction.

583
00:31:09,275 --> 00:31:12,875
The precise outcome depends on difficult details of stellar

584
00:31:12,875 --> 00:31:15,462
evolution and those interactions.

585
00:31:15,462 --> 00:31:19,375
The safe conclusion is not that every calculation guarantees

586
00:31:19,375 --> 00:31:21,425
the same final orbit.

587
00:31:21,425 --> 00:31:24,887
It is that orbital expansion alone does not guarantee

588
00:31:24,887 --> 00:31:26,325
Earth's survival.

589
00:31:26,775 --> 00:31:29,537
And survival of the rock would not mean survival of the

590
00:31:29,537 --> 00:31:33,475
home. A sterilized remnant on a changed orbit has not

591
00:31:33,475 --> 00:31:37,875
preserved the forests, coastlines, atmospheric chemistry,

592
00:31:37,875 --> 00:31:40,750
or living relationships that made Earth meaningful to

593
00:31:40,750 --> 00:31:41,550
us.

594
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:45,725
There are two endings here: the loss of a habitable world,

595
00:31:45,725 --> 00:31:48,362
and the possible destruction of the planet that once

596
00:31:48,362 --> 00:31:52,075
carried it. Confusing those endings can make the remaining

597
00:31:52,075 --> 00:31:55,187
lifetime of the biosphere appear much longer than the

598
00:31:55,187 --> 00:31:56,650
science supports.

599
00:31:57,700 --> 00:32:00,662
The Sun does not have enough mass to end its life as a

600
00:32:00,662 --> 00:32:02,537
core-collapse supernova.

601
00:32:02,537 --> 00:32:06,075
After its giant phases and the loss of outer material,

602
00:32:06,075 --> 00:32:10,012
it is expected to leave a white dwarf: a compact stellar

603
00:32:10,012 --> 00:32:12,350
remnant that gradually cools.

604
00:32:12,800 --> 00:32:15,762
That object would be the remains of the star that powered

605
00:32:15,762 --> 00:32:17,775
Earth's forests and weather.

606
00:32:17,775 --> 00:32:21,137
It would not offer today's solar system a return to its

607
00:32:21,137 --> 00:32:22,687
old arrangement.

608
00:32:22,687 --> 00:32:25,525
The planets and smaller bodies would have experienced

609
00:32:25,525 --> 00:32:28,775
a long, disruptive stellar transformation.

610
00:32:29,225 --> 00:32:32,212
We do not need to settle every detail of the far-future

611
00:32:32,212 --> 00:32:34,837
remnant to understand the central point.

612
00:32:34,837 --> 00:32:38,575
Earth's natural period of rich surface habitability is

613
00:32:38,575 --> 00:32:41,562
a chapter within a larger stellar history.

614
00:32:41,562 --> 00:32:43,875
The Sun existed before people.

615
00:32:43,875 --> 00:32:47,050
Its altered remnant could persist long after our planet's

616
00:32:47,050 --> 00:32:49,550
familiar environment has vanished.

617
00:32:50,025 --> 00:32:53,725
Looking that far ahead can make human history feel impossibly

618
00:32:53,725 --> 00:32:58,150
small. But duration is not the only measure of significance.

619
00:32:58,150 --> 00:33:01,375
A brief season can contain everything that matters to

620
00:33:01,375 --> 00:33:03,475
the lives experiencing it.

621
00:33:03,900 --> 00:33:07,525
The scientific question tells us how the stage changes.

622
00:33:07,525 --> 00:33:10,950
It cannot decide for us what the existence of the performance

623
00:33:10,950 --> 00:33:11,950
is worth.

624
00:33:13,025 --> 00:33:14,387
Let us pull back.

625
00:33:14,387 --> 00:33:17,900
Around a billion years is a useful scale in several studies

626
00:33:17,900 --> 00:33:21,737
of future carbon limitation and atmospheric oxygen.

627
00:33:21,737 --> 00:33:25,125
Other assumptions allow land plants to last substantially

628
00:33:25,125 --> 00:33:29,075
longer, toward roughly one point six to one point eight

629
00:33:29,075 --> 00:33:32,200
six billion years in the study we discussed.

630
00:33:32,650 --> 00:33:36,112
The future timing of severe greenhouse transitions and

631
00:33:36,112 --> 00:33:40,500
major water loss depends on climate modeling and atmospheric

632
00:33:40,500 --> 00:33:44,475
assumptions. Those transitions do not have to coincide

633
00:33:44,475 --> 00:33:48,025
with the first failure of human outdoor habitability.

634
00:33:48,500 --> 00:33:52,012
Around five billion years is a broad scale for the Sun

635
00:33:52,012 --> 00:33:54,362
moving toward its giant future.

636
00:33:54,362 --> 00:33:58,200
A later maximum expansion and possible engulfment of Earth

637
00:33:58,200 --> 00:34:01,987
belong to additional stages; the seven point six billion

638
00:34:01,987 --> 00:34:06,175
year result is tied to a particular stellar calculation.

639
00:34:06,800 --> 00:34:10,050
These are signposts from different investigations, not

640
00:34:10,050 --> 00:34:13,950
consecutive appointments copied from one master simulation.

641
00:34:13,950 --> 00:34:17,312
A billion years is also an enormous interval in which

642
00:34:17,312 --> 00:34:21,450
continents, organisms, and any surviving technological

643
00:34:21,450 --> 00:34:23,375
societies could change.

644
00:34:23,800 --> 00:34:26,837
The uncertainty is not a reason to replace the story with

645
00:34:26,837 --> 00:34:31,262
a shrug. The robust direction is clear: an evolving Sun

646
00:34:31,262 --> 00:34:34,575
eventually makes a naturally Earth-like home impossible

647
00:34:34,575 --> 00:34:36,937
at our planet's current situation.

648
00:34:36,937 --> 00:34:40,900
What remains uncertain is the detailed route, its timing,

649
00:34:40,900 --> 00:34:43,812
and any choices that future life might make along the

650
00:34:43,812 --> 00:34:44,675
way.

651
00:34:45,750 --> 00:34:49,175
Suppose a technological civilization survives long enough

652
00:34:49,175 --> 00:34:50,875
to confront this problem.

653
00:34:50,875 --> 00:34:55,175
Its first refuge might be local: sealed habitats, controlled

654
00:34:55,175 --> 00:34:59,525
agriculture, managed air, and protected supplies of water.

655
00:35:00,125 --> 00:35:03,337
We already operate limited life-support systems.

656
00:35:03,337 --> 00:35:06,775
That does not mean we have demonstrated a closed civilization

657
00:35:06,775 --> 00:35:10,562
that can reproduce, maintain its machinery, and preserve

658
00:35:10,562 --> 00:35:13,912
ecological stability for geological time.

659
00:35:13,912 --> 00:35:18,725
A habitat requires energy, materials, spare parts, knowledge,

660
00:35:18,725 --> 00:35:21,250
and institutions that keep functioning.

661
00:35:21,675 --> 00:35:23,912
It must also dump waste heat.

662
00:35:23,912 --> 00:35:26,875
A cooling machine does not make heat disappear.

663
00:35:26,875 --> 00:35:29,825
It transfers heat elsewhere while adding the energy used

664
00:35:29,825 --> 00:35:31,337
to run the machine.

665
00:35:31,337 --> 00:35:35,000
A steadily hotter environment makes that task harder.

666
00:35:35,450 --> 00:35:38,512
Underground construction can help with some environmental

667
00:35:38,512 --> 00:35:42,325
hazards. It does not grant immunity from the thermal state

668
00:35:42,325 --> 00:35:45,487
of the surrounding rock, or from the need to move heat

669
00:35:45,487 --> 00:35:47,125
to a suitable sink.

670
00:35:47,625 --> 00:35:51,225
Sealed living could extend human survival beyond the loss

671
00:35:51,225 --> 00:35:53,600
of comfortable outdoor conditions.

672
00:35:53,600 --> 00:35:56,212
It would also change what home means.

673
00:35:56,212 --> 00:35:59,212
The natural world outside would no longer provide its

674
00:35:59,212 --> 00:36:00,737
services freely.

675
00:36:00,737 --> 00:36:03,687
Every breath and meal would depend more visibly on an

676
00:36:03,687 --> 00:36:07,525
engineered system, and on the people entrusted with maintaining

677
00:36:07,525 --> 00:36:08,150
it.

678
00:36:09,200 --> 00:36:12,725
Another idea is to reduce the sunlight Earth receives.

679
00:36:12,725 --> 00:36:16,737
Proposals include space-based shades or many distributed

680
00:36:16,737 --> 00:36:20,525
objects arranged to deflect a small fraction of incoming

681
00:36:20,525 --> 00:36:21,325
light.

682
00:36:21,775 --> 00:36:24,837
The physics of blocking light is not mysterious.

683
00:36:24,837 --> 00:36:27,987
The engineering and governance of a planetary intervention

684
00:36:27,987 --> 00:36:29,487
are another matter.

685
00:36:29,487 --> 00:36:33,425
Construction, orbital behavior, maintenance, material

686
00:36:33,425 --> 00:36:36,962
supply, unintended climate effects, and the ability to

687
00:36:36,962 --> 00:36:40,500
stop or modify the system would all need attention.

688
00:36:40,975 --> 00:36:44,275
A shade would also address only part of the problem.

689
00:36:44,275 --> 00:36:47,875
Changing incoming radiation does not automatically restore

690
00:36:47,875 --> 00:36:52,425
the correct carbon supply, oxygen balance, nutrient cycles,

691
00:36:52,425 --> 00:36:55,012
or every regional ecosystem.

692
00:36:55,012 --> 00:36:58,625
A planet has more than one environmental control.

693
00:36:59,125 --> 00:37:02,625
No operating planetary sunshade currently demonstrates

694
00:37:02,625 --> 00:37:05,887
that we can preserve a biosphere against a progressively

695
00:37:05,887 --> 00:37:09,450
brighter star for hundreds of millions of years.

696
00:37:09,450 --> 00:37:13,375
These are speculative options to analyze, not services

697
00:37:13,375 --> 00:37:16,625
future generations are guaranteed to inherit.

698
00:37:17,075 --> 00:37:19,937
Still, the distinction between impossible physics and

699
00:37:19,937 --> 00:37:22,500
unproven engineering is useful.

700
00:37:22,500 --> 00:37:25,537
We should not dismiss every intervention because it sounds

701
00:37:25,537 --> 00:37:29,275
enormous. Nor should we treat a diagram with arrows as

702
00:37:29,275 --> 00:37:32,162
evidence that the hard parts have been solved.

703
00:37:32,162 --> 00:37:35,600
Saving a world requires more than identifying the direction

704
00:37:35,600 --> 00:37:37,675
in which a control should move.

705
00:37:38,700 --> 00:37:42,300
If the problem is too much sunlight, another thought experiment

706
00:37:42,300 --> 00:37:44,700
is to move Earth farther from the Sun.

707
00:37:44,700 --> 00:37:48,187
At greater distance, sunlight is spread over a larger

708
00:37:48,187 --> 00:37:49,125
area.

709
00:37:49,550 --> 00:37:53,200
For a simplified comparison, if solar luminosity rose

710
00:37:53,200 --> 00:37:56,950
by ten percent, maintaining the same incoming flux would

711
00:37:56,950 --> 00:38:00,487
require a distance larger by the square root of one point

712
00:38:00,487 --> 00:38:03,825
one: roughly five percent farther out.

713
00:38:03,825 --> 00:38:07,537
That calculation ignores the climate response and says

714
00:38:07,537 --> 00:38:10,275
nothing about how to move a planet safely.

715
00:38:10,700 --> 00:38:13,612
The orbital energy involved is immense.

716
00:38:13,612 --> 00:38:17,200
Any scheme would have to account for momentum, the Moon,

717
00:38:17,200 --> 00:38:20,225
the stability of other orbits, and the consequences

718
00:38:20,225 --> 00:38:24,125
of an error. Proposals involving repeated gravitational

719
00:38:24,125 --> 00:38:27,712
encounters are speculative studies, not a tested rescue

720
00:38:27,712 --> 00:38:28,675
system.

721
00:38:29,125 --> 00:38:32,525
Even a successful adjustment would buy time under particular

722
00:38:32,525 --> 00:38:36,725
conditions. It would not halt stellar evolution or guarantee

723
00:38:36,725 --> 00:38:39,837
the integrity of every part of the biosphere.

724
00:38:39,837 --> 00:38:43,575
The giant phases would create a much larger and more complicated

725
00:38:43,575 --> 00:38:44,600
problem.

726
00:38:45,075 --> 00:38:48,150
This is a useful boundary for imagination.

727
00:38:48,150 --> 00:38:51,875
We can calculate how an orbit affects incoming light.

728
00:38:51,875 --> 00:38:55,237
We cannot jump from that equation to the claim that humanity

729
00:38:55,237 --> 00:38:58,537
already possesses a practical plan to tow Earth away from

730
00:38:58,537 --> 00:38:59,675
its future.

731
00:39:00,750 --> 00:39:04,287
Migration offers a different answer: preserve the people

732
00:39:04,287 --> 00:39:07,887
somewhere else. That might mean constructed habitats within

733
00:39:07,887 --> 00:39:11,675
the solar system, or, much more speculatively, journeys

734
00:39:11,675 --> 00:39:13,175
to other stars.

735
00:39:13,700 --> 00:39:16,425
Mars is not a ready-made second Earth.

736
00:39:16,425 --> 00:39:19,837
Neither is a moon simply because it contains ice.

737
00:39:19,837 --> 00:39:23,150
A changing Sun could alter conditions farther out, but

738
00:39:23,150 --> 00:39:26,175
a region receiving a more favorable amount of light is

739
00:39:26,175 --> 00:39:29,700
not automatically supplied with breathable air, fertile

740
00:39:29,700 --> 00:39:33,175
soil, or a stable environment for humans.

741
00:39:33,625 --> 00:39:37,825
Interstellar travel adds distance, energy, radiation,

742
00:39:37,825 --> 00:39:40,362
life-support, and social challenges.

743
00:39:40,362 --> 00:39:43,875
A planet detected in a habitable zone is not a confirmed

744
00:39:43,875 --> 00:39:47,175
destination where passengers can open a hatch and walk

745
00:39:47,175 --> 00:39:47,925
out.

746
00:39:48,400 --> 00:39:51,737
As in our previous generation-ship story, transporting

747
00:39:51,737 --> 00:39:55,175
humans and preserving a living world are different tasks.

748
00:39:55,175 --> 00:39:57,262
Seeds are not a forest.

749
00:39:57,262 --> 00:40:00,950
A collection of frozen samples is not a functioning ocean.

750
00:40:00,950 --> 00:40:04,375
Even an enormous habitat would represent choices about

751
00:40:04,375 --> 00:40:07,300
what could be carried, supported, and allowed to keep

752
00:40:07,300 --> 00:40:08,375
evolving.

753
00:40:08,875 --> 00:40:11,900
Leaving could preserve a branch of Earth's story.

754
00:40:11,900 --> 00:40:15,137
It would not make the loss of Earth inconsequential.

755
00:40:15,137 --> 00:40:18,625
A new address can save lives while still leaving a home,

756
00:40:18,625 --> 00:40:20,850
and much of its life, behind.

757
00:40:21,925 --> 00:40:25,450
Imagine an archive prepared for a departure no one alive

758
00:40:25,450 --> 00:40:27,125
today could witness.

759
00:40:27,125 --> 00:40:30,700
It might contain genetic information, living cultures,

760
00:40:30,700 --> 00:40:34,950
seeds, scientific records, and instructions for maintaining

761
00:40:34,950 --> 00:40:37,000
the systems that support them.

762
00:40:37,425 --> 00:40:40,575
But instructions are not enough without readers who understand

763
00:40:40,575 --> 00:40:44,350
them, institutions that preserve them, and materials that

764
00:40:44,350 --> 00:40:46,225
allow them to be used.

765
00:40:46,225 --> 00:40:49,775
A library can survive physically while its practical meaning

766
00:40:49,775 --> 00:40:50,900
is lost.

767
00:40:51,375 --> 00:40:54,612
Some relationships are harder to store than individual

768
00:40:54,612 --> 00:40:59,275
specimens. Organisms depend on partners, habitats, and

769
00:40:59,275 --> 00:41:01,337
sequences of interaction.

770
00:41:01,337 --> 00:41:04,750
Preserving representative pieces does not automatically

771
00:41:04,750 --> 00:41:08,375
preserve the possibility of rebuilding the original whole.

772
00:41:09,000 --> 00:41:11,525
Then there are the things that matter to people without

773
00:41:11,525 --> 00:41:14,150
being necessary for basic survival.

774
00:41:14,150 --> 00:41:16,100
Languages. Music.

775
00:41:16,100 --> 00:41:20,175
Stories. Knowledge of what rain smelled like on soil.

776
00:41:20,175 --> 00:41:23,925
Memories of living among organisms that no one designed.

777
00:41:24,550 --> 00:41:28,200
Any future archive in this film is imagined, not a mission

778
00:41:28,200 --> 00:41:29,575
under construction.

779
00:41:29,575 --> 00:41:33,375
It asks a present question, though: when we say save humanity,

780
00:41:33,375 --> 00:41:37,075
how much of the larger living world do we mean to include?

781
00:41:37,075 --> 00:41:40,412
The answer changes the scale of the task, and the meaning

782
00:41:40,412 --> 00:41:41,675
of success.

783
00:41:42,750 --> 00:41:46,125
After a journey through billions of years, it is tempting

784
00:41:46,125 --> 00:41:49,537
to feel that everything nearby has become trivial.

785
00:41:49,537 --> 00:41:52,275
The opposite conclusion makes more sense.

786
00:41:52,725 --> 00:41:56,275
The solar changes discussed here are slow on the timescale

787
00:41:56,275 --> 00:41:58,062
of current societies.

788
00:41:58,062 --> 00:42:01,437
The rapid warming people are causing now is primarily

789
00:42:01,437 --> 00:42:05,312
driven by human greenhouse gas emissions, not by the Sun

790
00:42:05,312 --> 00:42:08,287
quietly approaching a red giant phase.

791
00:42:08,287 --> 00:42:11,150
Reducing that damage remains meaningful.

792
00:42:11,625 --> 00:42:14,825
A world that will eventually lose its oceans can still

793
00:42:14,825 --> 00:42:17,337
have an ocean worth protecting today.

794
00:42:17,337 --> 00:42:21,325
A species with a finite future can still avoid unnecessary

795
00:42:21,325 --> 00:42:25,775
extinction. Every generation lives inside a limited interval;

796
00:42:25,775 --> 00:42:28,500
that has never made care irrelevant.

797
00:42:28,950 --> 00:42:32,575
We also cannot assume that future technology will repair

798
00:42:32,575 --> 00:42:34,587
whatever we choose to break.

799
00:42:34,587 --> 00:42:38,125
The capabilities imagined in planetary rescue schemes

800
00:42:38,125 --> 00:42:39,887
are not guarantees.

801
00:42:39,887 --> 00:42:43,775
Keeping an existing biosphere healthy is a different proposition

802
00:42:43,775 --> 00:42:47,075
from reconstructing one after its relationships have been

803
00:42:47,075 --> 00:42:48,100
erased.

804
00:42:48,550 --> 00:42:51,687
The long future gives us perspective on the home we already

805
00:42:51,687 --> 00:42:55,237
have. It does not provide permission to neglect it.

806
00:42:55,237 --> 00:42:58,537
Between the birth of a habitable world and its eventual

807
00:42:58,537 --> 00:43:01,950
ending lies the only interval in which that particular

808
00:43:01,950 --> 00:43:04,350
living world can be experienced.

809
00:43:05,400 --> 00:43:07,700
Return to the shoreline from our opening.

810
00:43:07,700 --> 00:43:09,800
It does not need to be the final day.

811
00:43:09,800 --> 00:43:11,612
Perhaps it is simply today.

812
00:43:11,612 --> 00:43:14,687
A person stands beside water under a star that is still

813
00:43:14,687 --> 00:43:16,687
in the right stage of its life.

814
00:43:16,687 --> 00:43:19,900
The air contains oxygen because life and geology have

815
00:43:19,900 --> 00:43:22,812
made it so. Plants can obtain carbon.

816
00:43:22,812 --> 00:43:25,012
Clouds move heat and water.

817
00:43:25,012 --> 00:43:28,325
A planet-sized collection of processes keeps an ordinary

818
00:43:28,325 --> 00:43:29,600
breath possible.

819
00:43:30,100 --> 00:43:31,825
None of this is permanent.

820
00:43:31,825 --> 00:43:33,788
None of it was arranged for us.

821
00:43:33,788 --> 00:43:36,587
Yet here we are, able to understand at least part of the

822
00:43:36,587 --> 00:43:39,475
machinery, and to ask what comes after.

823
00:43:39,925 --> 00:43:43,062
Under natural stellar evolution, Earth will eventually

824
00:43:43,062 --> 00:43:46,550
stop being the home we recognize, long before the Sun

825
00:43:46,550 --> 00:43:48,112
completes its life.

826
00:43:48,112 --> 00:43:51,425
The exact milestones remain uncertain, and they differ

827
00:43:51,425 --> 00:43:54,762
for people, forests, oceans, and microbes.

828
00:43:54,762 --> 00:43:57,800
A surviving civilization might change the route.

829
00:43:57,800 --> 00:43:59,900
We do not know whether one will.

830
00:44:00,350 --> 00:44:03,250
What we do know is that a home can be temporary without

831
00:44:03,250 --> 00:44:04,825
being replaceable.

832
00:44:04,825 --> 00:44:08,175
Earth is not valuable because it can last forever.

833
00:44:08,175 --> 00:44:11,925
It is valuable because, for this extraordinary interval,

834
00:44:11,925 --> 00:44:15,450
a living world exists here, and we are among the lives

835
00:44:15,450 --> 00:44:16,700
it supports.

836
00:44:17,125 --> 00:44:18,925
The Sun will keep changing.

837
00:44:18,925 --> 00:44:21,562
Our part of the story is what we choose to do while the

838
00:44:21,562 --> 00:44:23,225
world is still blue.
